    "The deviation constraint has the wrong sign. It enforces t - early + late = target, which means t = target + early - late. Early and late are therefore swapped. The solution shows this: Aircraft 0 lands at 66, before its target of 71, yet it is charged late[0]=5 at $11 per minute instead of early at $15. Aircraft 2 lands at 71, before its target of 73, and is charged as late. The correct constraint is t - late + early = target, i.e. t - target = late - early. This changes the penalty coefficients applied to each deviation and so changes the optimal value."
  ],
  "assumptions": [
    "The separation for the reverse order (j before i) is taken to equal sep[(i,j)], i.e. the separation matrix is treated as symmetric, because the text lists only i<j pairs."
  ],
  "reason": "Earliness and lateness are swapped by a sign error in the deviation constraint, so the penalties are applied to the wrong deviations and the objective of 164 is not the intended optimum."
